codex.problem:
PT2 says "constant indices add their datalayout byte offsets" and represents the result as an offset in the original object, without the modular index computation that §16.4 now requires. This produces a false candidate set, not merely a different analysis precision. Witness:
 target datalayout = "e-p:64:64:64:32"
 @tab = internal constant [1 x ptr] [ptr @body]
 declare void @__bennett_adj(ptr, ptr, ptr)
 define internal void @body(ptr %e) {
   ret void
 }
 define void @top() {
   %p = getelementptr i8, ptr @tab, i64 4294967296
   %f = load ptr, ptr %p, align 8
   call void @__bennett_adj(ptr %f, ptr null, ptr null)
   ret void
 }
The index truncates to zero at I=32, so %p equals @tab, %f is @body and the adjoint invocation succeeds. There is no out-of-bounds access or source poison. LLVM 18 and 23 both verify this source, preserve it through default<O0>, and fold the marker operand to @body with instcombine. The repository recogniser instead returns accept with AV(%p)={(tab,4294967296)}, AV(%f)=empty and an empty candidate set. Under §7.2’s "No match is the run-time trap T-REGION (fault code 4)", that implementation traps on the defined invocation, whereas the finite-width reference executes @body. The correct §16.4 GEP map does not repair the earlier S2 candidate analysis.

N9 maps atomicrmw op to "%nz.v.r = <op> T %nz.l.r, v" and enumerates exceptions for xchg, min/max, FP and the round-5 wrap/subtract operations, but omits nand. LLVM has atomicrmw nand and no ordinary nand instruction, so the prescribed replacement is invalid IR. Retaining atomicrmw instead violates V6. Witness:
 @cell = internal global i8 -16, align 1
 define i8 @f() {
   %old = atomicrmw nand ptr @cell, i8 -52 seq_cst, align 1
   %new = load i8, ptr @cell, align 1
   ret i8 %new
 }
Both LLVM 18 and 23 verify and retain this source under default<O0>. Independent lli executions on both versions confirm returned old=240 and stored new=63, since ~(0xf0 & 0xcc) & 255 = 0x3f. The repository normaliser raises "N9 atomicrmw nand". Thus the normative recipe cannot deliver the promised sequentialised, liftable device function; an implementation must invent an expansion or reject a supported construct. Different invented expansions also change §16’s charged reference rows. This is a separate remaining opcode omission; the four operations reported by BD3-R5-4 are now fixed.
